In .NET 10 MAUI on Android, `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE` (which backed `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize`) is fully deprecated and no longer functions. The replacement is the **WindowInsets / edge-to-edge** model.

## Fix

**1. In `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`**, opt into edge-to-edge insets handling:

```csharp
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;
using Microsoft.Maui.Controls.PlatformConfiguration.AndroidSpecific;

[Activity(WindowSoftInputMode = SoftInput.AdjustResize, ...)]
public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity
{
    protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
    {
        base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

        // Enable edge-to-edge so insets (including IME) are dispatched
        WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window!, false);
    }
}
```

**2. In your page XAML**, wrap your chat layout so the input bar responds to keyboard insets:

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*,Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ItemsSource="{Binding Messages}" ... />

    <!-- Input bar -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="1" Padding="8">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." />
    </Grid>
</Grid>
```

**3. Apply keyboard padding** from the platform inset. In your page code-behind:

```csharp
#if ANDROID
using AndroidX.Core.Graphics;
using AndroidX.Core.View;

// In OnAppearing or after page loads:
var activity = Platform.CurrentActivity!;
ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
    activity.Window!.DecorView,
    new InsetsListener(this));

class InsetsListener(Page page) : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

        // Use the larger of IME or nav bar bottom inset
        double bottom = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom)
                        / DeviceDisplay.MainDisplayInfo.Density;

        page.Padding = new Thickness(0, 0, 0, bottom);
        return insets;
    }
}
#endif
```

## Why this works

- `SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false)` tells Android your app handles insets itself.
- `AdjustResize` still causes the window to resize for the IME, but now the **insets API** reports the keyboard height.
- You apply that height as bottom padding, pushing your input bar above the keyboard.

## iOS

On iOS this isn't needed — MAUI already adjusts for the keyboard automatically via `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll`. If you disabled it, re-enable with:

```csharp
// MauiProgram.cs
builder.ConfigureMauiHandlers(h =>
{
    Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Connect = true;
});
```
